<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="ku" dir="rtl">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    @* minimum-scale=1.0 is not decoration — it is what keeps every Telerik dropdown usable on an
       Android phone. Kendo measures a popup before it shows it: it drops the .k-animation-container
       into the page at `left: -10000px`, reads its size, and only then moves it under its anchor.
       That container is an ordinary absolutely-positioned element in the document, so for one frame
       the document is ten thousand pixels wide.

       iPhone shrugs that off. Chrome on Android answers a document far wider than the screen by
       zooming out to its minimum page scale, and at 0.25 the LAYOUT viewport of a 360px phone
       becomes 1440×2960. Everything else follows from that one number: the lg:/xl: breakpoints
       light up so the desktop sidebar appears, and the dialog re-centres itself in the middle of a
       viewport four times the screen and lands off it — leaving nothing on screen but the modal's
       grey overlay. Kendo then places the popup against that wrong geometry (left: -1080px), which
       keeps the document over-wide, so the browser never zooms back: the screen stays broken until
       something closes the popup, and a tap anywhere lands on the overlay, which is exactly what
       closes it.

       Pinning the minimum scale at 1 removes the zoom-out, so the measurement pass costs one
       invisible frame and nothing else. Zooming IN is untouched — no maximum-scale, no
       user-scalable=no — so this takes nothing away from anyone who needs to magnify the page. *@
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, minimum-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/NRT-Reg.ttf" as="font" type="font/truetype" crossorigin />
    @* Telerik's theme first, so our own tokens in app.css win every collision. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="_content/Telerik.UI.for.Blazor/css/kendo-theme-default/all.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/app.css?v=@AssetVersion" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="frontend-blazor.styles.css?v=@AssetVersion" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="favicon.png" />
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body class="min-h-screen bg-card">
    <Routes />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="_content/Telerik.UI.for.Blazor/js/telerik-blazor.js" defer></script>
    <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
    <script src="js/presence.js?v=@AssetVersion"></script>

    @* D3, vendored from node_modules by `npm run sync:d3` — the admin has no bundler, and the
       CDN is not an option on a network that may not reach it. A classic script, so it defines
       window.d3 before schema-map.js reads it; deferring either one would reintroduce the load
       order as a thing to get wrong. *@
    <script src="js/d3.min.js?v=@AssetVersion"></script>
    <script src="js/schema-map.js?v=@AssetVersion"></script>
</body>

</html>

@code {
    // Busts browser CSS cache on every deploy (assembly is rewritten on each build)
    private static readonly string AssetVersion =
        System.IO.File.GetLastWriteTimeUtc(typeof(App).Assembly.Location).Ticks.ToString();
}
